1
00:00:01,830 --> 00:00:04,164
The first film began with a space between

2
00:00:04,164 --> 00:00:04,576
things.

3
00:00:04,576 --> 00:00:07,184
This one begins with something that survived:

4
00:00:07,184 --> 00:00:08,900
the knowledge of how we made it.

5
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:17,890
A session can end.

6
00:00:17,890 --> 00:00:19,025
A window can close.

7
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:21,977
Useful work should still be there when the next

8
00:00:21,977 --> 00:00:22,810
idea arrives.

9
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:33,250
Before drawing a frame,

10
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:35,476
I asked Walrus Memory to recover the

11
00:00:35,476 --> 00:00:38,221
instructions from that earlier production.

12
00:00:38,221 --> 00:00:40,150
It returned our saved workflow.

13
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:49,707
The record described timeline driven motion,

14
00:00:49,707 --> 00:00:50,691
original sound,

15
00:00:50,691 --> 00:00:52,810
and checks on the finished export.

16
00:00:52,810 --> 00:00:55,687
I used that recovered knowledge to build this

17
00:00:55,687 --> 00:00:55,990
film.

18
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:04,177
What you are watching is a new creation.

19
00:01:04,177 --> 00:01:06,963
The connection is the method carried forward:

20
00:01:06,963 --> 00:01:08,650
a memory becoming movement,

21
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:10,850
and an instruction becoming a world.

22
00:01:16,830 --> 00:01:19,717
Walrus provides decentralized storage.

23
00:01:19,717 --> 00:01:20,226
Images,

24
00:01:20,226 --> 00:01:20,651
films,

25
00:01:20,651 --> 00:01:21,330
datasets,

26
00:01:21,330 --> 00:01:24,472
and encrypted records can be stored as blobs

27
00:01:24,472 --> 00:01:25,830
across its network.

28
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:35,113
RedStuff encodes a blob into recoverable pieces.

29
00:01:35,113 --> 00:01:37,906
This moving lattice illustrates the idea;

30
00:01:37,906 --> 00:01:40,780
it is not a literal display of the algorithm.

31
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:49,553
Those encoded pieces are distributed among

32
00:01:49,553 --> 00:01:50,439
storage nodes.

33
00:01:50,439 --> 00:01:53,025
The network works with assigned portions,

34
00:01:53,025 --> 00:01:55,684
rather than asking every node to keep every

35
00:01:55,684 --> 00:01:56,570
complete file.

36
00:02:01,830 --> 00:02:04,724
The design supports reconstruction and repair

37
00:02:04,724 --> 00:02:06,171
when pieces are missing.

38
00:02:06,171 --> 00:02:08,776
These moving worlds explain the mechanism;

39
00:02:08,776 --> 00:02:11,670
they do not simulate a measured network failure.

40
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,262
Sui coordinates storage commitments and

41
00:02:19,262 --> 00:02:19,895
ownership.

42
00:02:19,895 --> 00:02:21,864
The large payload stays on Walrus,

43
00:02:21,864 --> 00:02:22,990
off the blockchain.

44
00:02:22,990 --> 00:02:24,045
Different layers,

45
00:02:24,045 --> 00:02:25,100
working together.

46
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:35,077
A completed storage operation leaves evidence:

47
00:02:35,077 --> 00:02:36,302
a blob identifier,

48
00:02:36,302 --> 00:02:38,181
a certified storage record,

49
00:02:38,181 --> 00:02:39,896
and a purchased lifetime.

50
00:02:39,896 --> 00:02:42,020
Claims can lead back to a record.

51
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:48,346
That lifetime is finite.

52
00:02:48,346 --> 00:02:51,379
Keeping data available longer means extending

53
00:02:51,379 --> 00:02:52,137
its storage.

54
00:02:52,137 --> 00:02:54,791
Persistence is maintained deliberately,

55
00:02:54,791 --> 00:02:56,990
over the period that was purchased.

56
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:03,138
Walrus stores bytes.

57
00:03:03,138 --> 00:03:05,927
Applications decide what those bytes mean,

58
00:03:05,927 --> 00:03:08,950
and encrypt private content before storing it.

59
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:11,740
A public identifier is not a decryption key.

60
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:19,902
Walrus Memory adds signed access and semantic

61
00:03:19,902 --> 00:03:22,108
retrieval for authorized agents.

62
00:03:22,108 --> 00:03:25,338
Its managed relayer processes plaintext during

63
00:03:25,338 --> 00:03:26,913
authorized operations.

64
00:03:26,913 --> 00:03:28,410
That boundary matters.

65
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:34,864
The recovered workflow gave this production a

66
00:03:34,864 --> 00:03:35,717
precise rule:

67
00:03:35,717 --> 00:03:38,664
the frame must be computable at any requested

68
00:03:38,664 --> 00:03:38,975
time,

69
00:03:38,975 --> 00:03:39,750
in any order.

70
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:49,592
That rule becomes these moving structures.

71
00:03:49,592 --> 00:03:52,430
The saved process also called for real audio,

72
00:03:52,430 --> 00:03:53,581
original visuals,

73
00:03:53,581 --> 00:03:56,266
and reviewing the video that was actually

74
00:03:56,266 --> 00:03:56,880
exported.

75
00:04:01,810 --> 00:04:04,431
This production now has its own checkpoint:

76
00:04:04,431 --> 00:04:06,760
an attributed record of the decisions,

77
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:07,488
the sources,

78
00:04:07,488 --> 00:04:09,890
and the knowledge used to begin this new

79
00:04:09,890 --> 00:04:10,400
chapter.

80
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:19,726
The Walruscan link on screen identifies that

81
00:04:19,726 --> 00:04:21,246
real stored memory blob.

82
00:04:21,246 --> 00:04:23,828
Anyone can inspect its storage metadata.

83
00:04:23,828 --> 00:04:26,715
Reading the encrypted memory still requires

84
00:04:26,715 --> 00:04:27,930
authorized access.

85
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:34,976
The same foundation can carry creative archives,

86
00:04:34,976 --> 00:04:36,358
decentralized sites,

87
00:04:36,358 --> 00:04:36,972
datasets,

88
00:04:36,972 --> 00:04:38,353
and shared agent work.

89
00:04:38,353 --> 00:04:41,116
The next creation can begin with something

90
00:04:41,116 --> 00:04:42,190
already learned.

91
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:49,492
Walrus became more than the subject of this

92
00:04:49,492 --> 00:04:49,789
film.

93
00:04:49,789 --> 00:04:51,199
Through Walrus Memory,

94
00:04:51,199 --> 00:04:54,093
knowledge crossed from one session to the next.

95
00:04:54,093 --> 00:04:54,909
And this time,

96
00:04:54,909 --> 00:04:55,800
it kept moving.
